<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Experimental Results</h2>

<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.26788v1/bf16_vs_fp16_eval_zxx.svg" id="A2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/281;" width="476" height="281"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Evaluation comparisons between BF16 and FP16 across various frameworks, algorithms, datasets and training regimes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <a href="#S0.F1" title="In Defeating the Training-Inference Mismatch via FP16" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the training reward curves under different precisions, <a href="#A2.F6" title="In Appendix B More Experimental Results ‣ Defeating the Training-Inference Mismatch via FP16" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows evaluation results using checkpoints trained with these precisions. The results indicate that FP16-trained models generalize well to unseen benchmarks, further supporting our claim.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
